<div class="sasha-practice-page">

  <div class="sasha-container">


    <!-- ============================================
         HEADER
    ============================================= -->

    <header class="sasha-practice-page-hero">

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-eyebrow">
        Sasha Best Practice
      </p>

      <h1>
        Red Team Review
      </h1>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-intro">
        Review the substantially complete proposal from
        the evaluator's point of view. A Red Team Review
        asks whether the submission is compliant,
        responsive, persuasive, easy to evaluate, and
        competitive enough to submit.
      </p>

    </header>


    <!-- ============================================
         WHAT IS RED TEAM
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          What is a Red Team Review?
        </h2>

        <p>
          A Red Team Review evaluates the proposal when it
          is substantially complete.
        </p>

        <p>
          By this stage, the win strategy, work plan,
          proposed team, evidence, and major proposal
          structure should already be established.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Red Team's job is to experience the proposal
          as the client will experience it and determine
          whether it competes effectively.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            The central question
          </strong>

          <span>
            Would this proposal compete effectively if
            submitted in its current form?
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         NOT PROOFREADING
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Red Team is not proofreading
        </h2>

        <p>
          A Red Team Review is not primarily an exercise
          in finding typos, punctuation errors, or minor
          formatting problems.
        </p>

        <p>
          Those details still matter, but they belong to
          proofreading and final production.
        </p>

        <p>
          Red Team asks whether the proposal can win.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            Keep the distinction clear
          </strong>

          <span>
            Red Team asks whether the proposal is
            competitive. Proofreading asks whether it is
            correct.
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEWERS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Use fresh reviewers
        </h2>

        <p>
          Whenever possible, use reviewers who have enough
          distance from the pursuit to react like an
          evaluator rather than like a proposal contributor.
        </p>

        <p>
          People who have worked on the proposal for days
          or weeks may understand arguments, acronyms,
          assumptions, and relationships that the actual
          evaluator will not.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Impartial reviewers
            </strong>

            <span>
              Read the proposal with fresh eyes and judge
              only what is actually on the page.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Technical reviewers
            </strong>

            <span>
              Test methodology, technical credibility,
              risk response, and project understanding.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Proposal reviewers
            </strong>

            <span>
              Test structure, responsiveness, evaluator
              experience, clarity, and strategic messaging.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Sasha
            </strong>

            <span>
              Performs a detailed parallel review against
              the RFP, strategy, evidence, requirements,
              consistency, and commitments.
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEW PACKAGE
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Prepare the reviewers properly
        </h2>

        <p>
          Give reviewers the materials they need to judge
          the proposal against the competition rather than
          simply offering personal reactions.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>RFP and relevant addenda</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Mandatory requirements</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Evaluation criteria and scoring</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Current win strategy</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Substantially complete proposal draft</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Relevant assumptions and commitments</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Previous review findings</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Known unresolved issues</span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEW STEPS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <h2>
          Conduct the Red Team Review
        </h2>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-step-list">


        <!-- STEP 1 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            01
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Test compliance
            </h3>

            <p>
              Confirm that every mandatory requirement is
              clearly and completely addressed.
            </p>

            <p>
              Look for:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                missing forms;
              </li>

              <li>
                unanswered requirements;
              </li>

              <li>
                incorrect file or submission requirements;
              </li>

              <li>
                page-limit problems;
              </li>

              <li>
                missing signatures;
              </li>

              <li>
                mandatory attachments;
              </li>

              <li>
                incorrect terminology; and
              </li>

              <li>
                anything that could cause disqualification.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 2 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            02
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Review against the evaluation criteria
            </h3>

            <p>
              Where scoring criteria are available, use
              them.
            </p>

            <p>
              Reviewers should be able to explain why a
              section would or would not earn strong marks.
            </p>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                Make comments specific
              </strong>

              <span>
                "I didn't like this section" is not useful.
                Explain which criterion is weak and why.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 3 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            03
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Test responsiveness
            </h3>

            <p>
              Determine whether the proposal directly
              answers what the client asked.
            </p>

            <p>
              Watch for:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                answers buried beneath background;
              </li>

              <li>
                sections that paraphrase the RFP without
                adding insight;
              </li>

              <li>
                content that sounds relevant but does not
                answer the criterion; and
              </li>

              <li>
                important information placed where the
                evaluator is unlikely to find it.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 4 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            04
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Test the win strategy
            </h3>

            <p>
              The proposal should still reflect the
              strategy developed earlier in the pursuit.
            </p>

            <p>
              Ask:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                Are the core win themes visible?
              </li>

              <li>
                Do they appear throughout the proposal
                without becoming repetitive?
              </li>

              <li>
                Does the proposal reinforce the reasons
                this team should be selected?
              </li>

              <li>
                Has the strategy weakened or disappeared
                during writing?
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 5 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            05
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Test project understanding
            </h3>

            <p>
              Determine whether the proposal demonstrates
              insight rather than merely repeating the
              client's scope.
            </p>

            <p>
              The evaluator should be able to see:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                what makes the project difficult;
              </li>

              <li>
                what risks matter;
              </li>

              <li>
                what must go right;
              </li>

              <li>
                how the proposed approach responds; and
              </li>

              <li>
                why that response is appropriate.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 6 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            06
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Review the methodology
            </h3>

            <p>
              Test whether the methodology tells a credible
              story of future project delivery.
            </p>

            <p>
              Review:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                task logic;
              </li>

              <li>
                objectives;
              </li>

              <li>
                activities;
              </li>

              <li>
                deliverables;
              </li>

              <li>
                dependencies;
              </li>

              <li>
                client involvement;
              </li>

              <li>
                multidisciplinary coordination;
              </li>

              <li>
                risk responses;
              </li>

              <li>
                schedule consistency; and
              </li>

              <li>
                alignment with the win strategy.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 7 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            07
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Review the proposed team
            </h3>

            <p>
              Determine whether the proposal clearly
              explains why the proposed people are right
              for the assignment.
            </p>

            <p>
              Watch for:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                biographies built around years of
                experience rather than relevant work;
              </li>

              <li>
                unclear roles;
              </li>

              <li>
                inconsistent titles;
              </li>

              <li>
                important specialists missing from the
                methodology;
              </li>

              <li>
                team members included without a clear
                purpose; and
              </li>

              <li>
                claims about experience that exceed the
                evidence.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 8 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            08
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Review project and personnel evidence
            </h3>

            <p>
              Strong claims require strong evidence.
            </p>

            <p>
              Ask:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                Are project examples genuinely comparable?
              </li>

              <li>
                Is the relevance clearly explained?
              </li>

              <li>
                Did the proposed people perform the roles
                being claimed?
              </li>

              <li>
                Are important results quantified where
                evidence exists?
              </li>

              <li>
                Does each example strengthen the proposal?
              </li>

            </ul>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                Evidence test
              </strong>

              <span>
                Do not include something merely because it
                is impressive. Include it because it helps
                the evaluator believe the argument.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 9 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            09
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Test differentiation
            </h3>

            <p>
              Look for vague claims such as:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                extensive experience;
              </li>

              <li>
                innovative solutions;
              </li>

              <li>
                multidisciplinary expertise;
              </li>

              <li>
                client-focused approach;
              </li>

              <li>
                proven methods; and
              </li>

              <li>
                commitment to quality.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <p>
              Replace generic claims with specific evidence
              and proposal-specific reasons for selection.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 10 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            10
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Review the evaluator experience
            </h3>

            <p>
              The proposal should be easy to read, skim,
              navigate, and score.
            </p>

            <p>
              Check:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                headings;
              </li>

              <li>
                section organization;
              </li>

              <li>
                page density;
              </li>

              <li>
                tables;
              </li>

              <li>
                callouts;
              </li>

              <li>
                graphics;
              </li>

              <li>
                captions;
              </li>

              <li>
                visual hierarchy;
              </li>

              <li>
                acronyms; and
              </li>

              <li>
                whether important answers are easy to find.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 11 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            11
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check consistency
            </h3>

            <p>
              A proposal written by many people must still
              read like one document.
            </p>

            <p>
              Look for inconsistencies in:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                names;
              </li>

              <li>
                project titles;
              </li>

              <li>
                team roles;
              </li>

              <li>
                terminology;
              </li>

              <li>
                methodology;
              </li>

              <li>
                schedule commitments;
              </li>

              <li>
                deliverables;
              </li>

              <li>
                assumptions;
              </li>

              <li>
                win themes; and
              </li>

              <li>
                client and project references.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 12 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            12
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check commitments
            </h3>

            <p>
              Proposal wording can create contractual
              commitments.
            </p>

            <p>
              Review language involving:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                scope;
              </li>

              <li>
                schedule;
              </li>

              <li>
                personnel;
              </li>

              <li>
                meetings;
              </li>

              <li>
                deliverables;
              </li>

              <li>
                guarantees;
              </li>

              <li>
                outcomes;
              </li>

              <li>
                client responsibilities; and
              </li>

              <li>
                assumptions or exclusions.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <p>
              Make sure the proposal does not promise more
              than the firm intends or is prepared to
              deliver.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 13 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            13
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Identify unresolved gaps
            </h3>

            <p>
              Look for anything that still weakens the
              proposal materially.
            </p>

            <p>
              Examples include:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                unsupported claims;
              </li>

              <li>
                missing evidence;
              </li>

              <li>
                unanswered requirements;
              </li>

              <li>
                contradictory sections;
              </li>

              <li>
                confusing graphics;
              </li>

              <li>
                weak headings;
              </li>

              <li>
                buried benefits;
              </li>

              <li>
                excessive repetition;
              </li>

              <li>
                missing project-specific insight; and
              </li>

              <li>
                content that does not help the evaluator.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         PRIORITIES
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Prioritize the findings
        </h2>

        <p>
          Not every reviewer comment deserves the same
          attention.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Critical
            </strong>

            <span>
              Could cause disqualification, unacceptable
              risk, a major strategic failure, or failure
              to answer an important criterion.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Major
            </strong>

            <span>
              Materially weakens competitiveness,
              credibility, evidence, clarity, or evaluator
              understanding.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Moderate
            </strong>

            <span>
              Improves the proposal and should be addressed
              where practical.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Minor
            </strong>

            <span>
              Editorial, stylistic, formatting, or
              preference-based comments with limited
              competitive impact.
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            Prioritize problems
          </strong>

          <span>
            Do not allow twenty minor comments to obscure
            one critical finding.
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         SASHA ROLE
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div>

          <h2>
            Sasha's Red Team contribution
          </h2>

        </div>

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Sasha can perform a detailed Red Team review
            alongside human reviewers.
          </p>

          <p>
            She can compare the proposal against mandatory
            requirements, evaluation criteria, RFP
            terminology, the win strategy, project and
            personnel evidence, source documents,
            commitments, readability, and consistency.
          </p>

          <p>
            She can identify unsupported claims,
            contradictions, generic language, buried
            answers, weak evidence, missing benefits,
            excessive density, confusing graphics,
            unexplained acronyms, and inconsistent names,
            dates, roles, or project information.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sasha complements impartial human review.
            She does not replace it.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         ACTIONS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Turn findings into actions
        </h2>

        <p>
          A review has little value if the findings remain
          in meeting notes.
        </p>

        <p>
          For each significant finding, record:
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Issue</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Priority</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Section affected</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Action required</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Responsible person</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Due date</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Status</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Confirmation that the issue was resolved</span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         FINAL TEST
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Is the proposal ready to move forward?
        </h2>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Mandatory requirements are addressed.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Evaluation criteria are easy to score.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              The proposal is responsive to the RFP.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Win themes remain visible.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Project understanding is convincing.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              The methodology is credible and consistent.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Team and project evidence support the claims.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Important answers are easy to find.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Important inconsistencies are resolved.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              No critical Red Team findings remain open.
            </span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
